				Catalogs.DOC


This document describes the various formats of nomenclature understood by the
various catalogs available to xsky.  These formats determine the label placed
on the object in the sky display, and determine the names which will be under-
stood by the Find option.

Labels are generated by code in the catalog specific to the object selected.
The Find option, however, has no way of knowing which catalog the entered
designation is chosen from.  Therefore, it asks each catalog, in turn, if it
understands the given designation.

(This is done even for unselected catalogs; this means that you can find 61
Cygni, through the auspices of the YBS or BSC5 catalogs, if available, even
if they are not on the display.  This feature is useful when displaying cata-
logs such as the SAO, which has only a limited capacity for star designations.)

Constellations may be entered as a fully-spelled-out Latin genitive-case name,
such as "Ursae Majoris", or given by their three-letter abbreviation, such as
"LMi" for Leo Minor.  The Latin genitive-case names and the three-letter ab-
breviations recognized by xsky can be found in the header file constell.h.



YBS (the Yale Catalog of Bright Stars, 4th ed.):

Label:

This catalog numbers its stars with HR numbers.  However, I've chosen to prefer
the Bayer designation (Greek letter) as the primary identification for these
stars, followed by the Flamsteed number.  If the star has neither of these,
the Henry Draper number will be used, if available.  After that, the Bonner
Durchmusterung designation will be used.  Only if none of those are available
will the HR number be used.

Find formats:  HR 1999   YBS 1999   alpha Ori   beta Ursae Majoris   61 Cygni

The letters "HR" or "YBS", in either upper or lower case, will indicate to
Find that an HR number is being specified.  A number followed by a constella-
tion abbreviation or genitive-case constellation name will be understood as
a Flamsteed number.  A Greek letter followd by a constellation will be inter-
preted as a Bayer designation.  The names of the Greek letters must be entered
in lower case.

Additionally, star names given in starnames.dat can be used.



RNGC (the Revised New General Catalog of Non-Stellar Objects):

Label:

Objects will be preferentially labelled by their Messier number, if any, in
the format "M 31".  Otherwise, the RNGC number will be used, as "NGC 7293".

Find formats:  M 33   NGC 205   RNGC 4565

An "M" indicates a Messier number; either "NGC" or "RNGC" indicates an RNGC
number.  The "M", "NGC", and "RNGC" can be entered in either upper or lower
case.

Additionally, object names given in ngc_names.dat can be used, except for
the ones which are IC catalog objects.



QSO (the Revised Optical Catalog of Quasi-Stellar Objects):

Label:

QSOs are labelled by their position-determined designation in this catalog.

Find format:  QSO 1317-277

Find understands only the catalog designations of these objects.  "QSO" may
appear in either upper or lower case.



SAO (the Smithsonian Astrophysical Observatory Star Catalog):

Label:

The SAO number is used, as in "SAO 252838".

Find format:  SAO 258857

The letters "SAO", in either upper or lower case, indicate that an SAO number
is upcoming.



VAR (the General Catalog of Variable Stars):

Label:

Stars from this catalog are preferentially labelled with their Bayer desig-
nation, but most of them carry Argelander designations such as VV Pup, or
ordinal designations, such as V564 Oph.

Find formats:  RR Lyrae   V453 Sco   delta Cephei

Argelander and ordinal designations can be used, as well as Bayer designations.

There is an ambiguity between the Greek letters mu and nu and the Argelander
designations MU and NU.  To resolve this ambiguity, the Greek letters must
be spelled in all lower case, and the Argelander designations must be given
in all upper case.



DBL (the Washington Catalog of Double Stars);

Label:

Stars in this catalog are labelled with the record number from the catalog,
as WDS 38893.

Find format:  WDS 56170

The "WDS" may be entered in either upper or lower case.



BSC5 (the Bright Star Catalog, 5th revised edition):

This catalog is a follow-on addition to the fourth edition of the Yale Catalog
of Bright Stars.  Stars are labelled as in YBS.  The Find formats accepted are
the same, substituting "BSC" or "BSC5", in either upper or lower case, for the
letters "YBS".



SAOJ2000 (the Smithsonian Astrophysical Observatory Star Catalog, J2000):

This catalog is an update to the SAO catalog.  It generates identical labels
and accepts the identical Find format.



GCVS (the General Catalog of Variable Stars):

This is the CD-ROM version of the VAR catalog, documented above.



WDS (the Washington Catalog of Double Stars):

This is the CD-ROM version of the DBL catalog, documented above.



NGC2000 (the New General Catalog of Nebulae and Clusters - 2000.0):

Label:

Objects will be preferentially labelled by their Messier number, if any,
in the format "M 82".  Otherwise, the NGC or IC number will be used, as
"NGC 2632" or "IC 434".

Find formats:  M 101   NGC 2237   IC 5146

An "M" indicates a Messier number; "NGC" and "IC" indicate an NGC or IC
number, respectively.  The "M", "NGC", or "IC" can be entered in either
upper or lower case.

Additionally, object names given in ngc_names.dat can be used.



UGC (the Washington Catalog of Double Stars);

Label:

Stars in this catalog are labelled with the record number from the catalog,
as UGC 11043.

Find format:  UGC 7456

The "UGC" may be entered in either upper or lower case.



User (user-defined catalog):

Label:

Each object is labelled with the name given in the label field of the user
catalog.

Find formats:  USER 1   label

The word "USER", in either upper or lower case, indicates a user object
number.  Objects may also be found by the given label.  However, if the
given label matches a star name from starnames.dat, that star will be
"found" instead, if either the YBS or BSC5 catalogs are available.  This
occurs because the user catalog is always searched last for object iden-
tifications.
